[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There\'s a second person using my computer right now, moving my cursor, typing in my apps, and filling in my spreadsheets. I plugged them in myself, and I\'m still the one who decides what they\'re allowed to touch. First, I thought it\'s a chat box in a nicer'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'jacket, but I was wrong in a way that I did not see coming. AI got unbelievably smart, and I\'m still sitting here doing tasks where I\'m literally thinking an LLM could do this in seconds, probably better than I would. So, why am I'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spending my afternoon on it instead of spending a couple of euros in tokens? This is the first thing I\'ve seen that tries to fix that instead of just being another smarter AI chat. It\'s small, 450 g, and there\'s a little touch screen on'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the front, a physical key on the top, and remember that key. It matters later. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; And on the back, there\'s HDMI in, USB out, and power delivery in. That\'s the whole setup.'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, so what is this thing, really? The simplest way I can put this, it\'s a second user for my computer, a little robot whose entire job is to sit there and use the'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'machine I already own. The HDMI cable lets it see my screen. The USB gives it my keyboard and mouse. It runs on its own processor, so it isn\'t borrowing anything from my computer. And instead'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of waiting for prompt, it works out what I\'m probably trying to do and prepares the next steps before I ask. Then it stops. Nothing happens until I press that key. With every other AI, you start'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by writing a prompt. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; This one starts by just reading your screen. Reading what you\'re looking at. So, my big goal was to automate all my repetitive tasks, the one you probably'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have, too, and see if it could actually take them off my plate. Before any of that works, it has to actually reach my tools, my Gmail, my Notion, etc. And this is usually the part some people'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'already check out. Connectors, [music] API keys, the stuff that makes normal people quit. Except, I basically didn\'t do it. I told it to connect my Gmail, and it opened up a'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'browser and just did it. It clicked the buttons, filled the fields, walked [music] through the whole setup on its own. You really need zero experience for this. The only moment it stopped was the'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one part that should stop. My password, my API keys, and the secrets. For those, it asked permission. Everything else, it handled. Same story with Notion. It connected everything itself, from start'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to finish. And here\'s a trick that worked scary well. My Notion is a mess only I understand. So, I asked Claude, which I\'ve been using for months, to write me a full prompt explaining how my'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Notion is actually structured, my CRM, my columns, the whole logic. I handed that one paragraph to Vivaldi Loop, and suddenly it got my system. Before we are going to do the first test, you have to'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go to ChatGPT or Claude or whatever you\'re using daily or weekly, and just tell it to write a full prompt for your AI agent &gt;&gt; [music]'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; to know everything about you. So, it should include all information about what you do, who you are, and [music] what your current projects are, for example. And this full prompt that Claude, for example, gives you, you just put it in Vivaldi Loop, and Vivaldi Loop'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will build the memory around it. The big brain. And this is a really interesting part about Vivaldi Loop because it continues to learn and builds this beautiful net of everything it knows about you. And you have a great'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'overview. You can also delete different steps or different memories, which is great, but it\'s it just keeps growing and learning by reading your screen. One thing I wanted to automate was, whenever'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I read an email, Vivaldi Loop should understand the context and update my Notion database. But, Vialoop was pretty honest about its capabilities, and it said, "Hey,'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automations can only be done with automated tasks." So, every 2 minutes, every 1 hour, but I wanted it to be live. And this is where I understood one thing. The memory is'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the core thing about Vialoop, because Vialoop now put it into its memory, "Hey, when the user reads an email, check if you could update the Notion database." And now, it still works. It\'s'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automated. Whenever I read an email about a collaboration, Vialoop comes up and says, "Hey, this is about a collaboration. Should I update the Notion database?" And this made me realize I can just do anything I'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could imagine with this agent. And what I did next was I told Vialoop, "Hey, memorize. Whenever I\'m in YouTube Studio and uploading a video or editing a video,'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'offer me to generate title and description options." And it worked. I opened up YouTube Studio to try it. I edited a video,'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and like 5 seconds later, there was this pop-up. "Hey, you\'re you\'re in YouTube Studio. Should I help you?" And I was like, "Yes, go for it." And I was just sitting there with the same feeling that I had'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when I used ChatGPT for the first time. The biggest difference between Vialoop and Claude is that Vialoop can proactively watch your screen 24/7 and help you out. Claude doesn\'t work 24/7.'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s based on prompts that will run for 1 minute, 15 minutes, maybe 30 minutes, but Vialoop is the agent that\'s here for you all the time. Here\'s a funny example. At one point, Vialoop'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recognized, "Okay, I\'m the owner of Create Beyond, which is an e-learning community. And then, whenever I visited other e-learning communities, it offered me to run a comparison. Hey, let\'s'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compare your business to this business. And when I said, "Okay, let\'s do it." It really built a whole table comparing the pricing, all the content, and everything. Even when I just was on the'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'story Storytelling Hero, no, Story Hero Academy of my friend Nicholas, it just it just popped up the Vivaldi Loop notification. So, running this for your business can be so helpful in many ways.'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Another cool example was when I was shopping for some accessories. I was on a website and Vivaldi Loop just said, "Hey, okay, you\'re shopping for this product. Should I compare this product to some other products?" And I was like,'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '"Yes, that could actually save me money, find me better options, and deliver the best result, the best product for my needs because you know everything about me, right?" I just started the upload of'

